From: CoolFreeze23 <34906576+CoolFreeze23@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath12k: take the reset request and clear the answer atomically

The reset work consumed ATH12K_FLAG_RESET_REQUESTED and then cleared
ATH12K_FLAG_RESET_ANSWERED as two operations. A group power-up between
them (clear the request, set the answer) had its answer cleared, so the
run later reset a device that had been booted after its request.
Swapping the two is not enough either: a boot between "clear the answer"
and "consume the request" leaves an answer that predates a request made
after it, and the run then skips a real crash.

Take the request and clear the answer in one try_cmpxchg() on
dev_flags.
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
@@ -1875,6 +1875,30 @@ static void ath12k_core_reset_skipped(st
 	mutex_unlock(&ag->mutex);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Take the pending reset request, and clear the answer of earlier group
+ * power-ups, in one atomic step. As two steps either order loses: clear
+ * then consume keeps the answer of a boot made between the two, before a
+ * request that arrives after it; consume then clear drops the answer of a
+ * boot made in between, after the request. The group power-up clears the
+ * request and then sets the answer, so a boot is either before the take
+ * (the request is gone: stale run) or after it (its answer stays).
+ */
+static bool ath12k_core_reset_take_request(struct ath12k_base *ab)
+{
+	unsigned long old = READ_ONCE(ab->dev_flags);
+	unsigned long new;
+
+	do {
+		if (!(old & BIT(ATH12K_FLAG_RESET_REQUESTED)))
+			return false;
+		new = old & ~(BIT(ATH12K_FLAG_RESET_REQUESTED) |
+			      BIT(ATH12K_FLAG_RESET_ANSWERED));
+	} while (!try_cmpxchg(&ab->dev_flags, &old, new));
+
+	return true;
+}
+
 static void ath12k_core_reset(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	struct ath12k_base *ab = container_of(work, struct ath12k_base, reset_work);
@@ -1889,11 +1913,10 @@ static void ath12k_core_reset(struct wor
 	 * since. Resetting the device again would tear down a recovery in
 	 * progress or a group that is already up.
 	 */
-	if (!test_and_clear_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_RESET_REQUESTED, &ab->dev_flags)) {
+	if (!ath12k_core_reset_take_request(ab)) {
 		ath12k_dbg(ab, ATH12K_DBG_BOOT, "no reset requested, stale reset work\n");
 		return;
 	}
-	clear_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_RESET_ANSWERED, &ab->dev_flags);
 
 	/*
 	 * The MHI RDDM callback tests UNREGISTERING without ag->mutex, so a
